+    id: "faq-1",
+    question: "Why do cats purr?",
+    answer: `Cats purr for a surprisingly wide range of reasons. While we often associate purring with contentment, cats also purr when they are anxious, injured, or even giving birth. The vibration frequency (typically 25–150 Hz) has been linked to <strong>promoting bone density and healing</strong>. Think of it as a built-in self-soothing mechanism — and a very pleasant one for their humans, too.`,
+  },
+  {
+    id: "faq-2",
+    question: "How much do cats sleep each day?",
+    answer: `Cats are champions of rest, sleeping anywhere from <strong>12 to 16 hours per day</strong> — and some senior cats snooze up to 20 hours. They are crepuscular by nature, meaning they are most active at dawn and dusk. Short, repeated naps (the famous "cat naps") help them conserve energy between bursts of hunting-style activity.`,
+  },
+  {
+    id: "faq-3",
+    question: "Do cats always land on their feet?",
+    answer: `Cats possess a remarkable <strong>righting reflex</strong> that allows them to rotate their body mid-air and land feet-first most of the time. This reflex develops by around 7 weeks of age. However, "always" is an overstatement — falls from very short heights may not give them enough time to correct, and injuries can still occur from great heights. Always keep windows screened!`,
+  },
+  {
+    id: "faq-4",
+    question: "Why do cats knock things off tables?",
+    answer: `This beloved behaviour has a few explanations:
+    <ul>
+      <li><strong>Curiosity & exploration</strong> — they use their paws to investigate objects and learn their properties.</li>
+      <li><strong>Attention-seeking</strong> — they quickly learn that pushing your mug causes a reaction from you.</li>
+      <li><strong>Predatory instinct</strong> — batting at moving (or potentially moving) objects mimics hunting behaviour.</li>
+    </ul>
+    In short: it's mostly intentional, and they enjoy it.`,
+  },
+  {
+    id: "faq-5",
+    question: "Can cats see in complete darkness?",
+    answer: `Not in <em>total</em> darkness, but cats can see in light levels roughly <strong>six times lower</strong> than humans can perceive. Their eyes have a higher rod-to-cone ratio for superior low-light sensitivity, elliptical pupils that open very wide, and a reflective layer behind the retina called the <strong>tapetum lucidum</strong> — which is what causes the eerie eye-shine you see in photos.`,
+  },
+  {
+    id: "faq-6",
+    question: "Why do cats bring dead animals as gifts?",
+    answer: `This behaviour is rooted in <strong>maternal instinct</strong>. In the wild, mother cats bring prey to their kittens to teach them hunting skills. Your cat likely considers you a beloved but helpless family member in need of hunting lessons. Some cats also bring toys or other objects for the same reason. The best response? Praise them — they mean well.`,
+  },
+  {
+    id: "faq-7",
+    question: "What does slow blinking mean?",
+    answer: `A slow blink from a cat is often called a <strong>"cat kiss"</strong> — it's a signal of trust and relaxed affection. When a cat holds eye contact and then slowly closes and reopens their eyes, they're communicating that they feel safe and comfortable with you. You can return the gesture: make eye contact, smile softly, and blink slowly. Many cats will blink back.`,
